  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. KEDEM",
      "case_id": "2016-15-7250",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "b503684a355fd1e8fd71f3fbba7e862b633a40281c24243c07421c671bf3e978",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b503684a355f-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "1 MR. KEDEM: The rule there is you have to\n2 file a second notice of appeal. And I'd point you to a\n3 case called Ray Haluch Gravel. That's a good example\n4 where you might have a judgment for the defendants on\n5 summary judgment and then a notice of appeal. And at\n6 the time it grants summary judgment, the district court\n7 says, I'm going to determine at a later time the\n8 appropriate amount of attorneys' fees.\n9 You cannot, on the basis of the first notice\n10 of appeal, challenge the amount of attorneys' fees. You\n11 have to file a second notice of appeal.\n12 We also think that --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. KEDEM",
      "case_id": "2016-15-7250",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "b503684a355fd1e8fd71f3fbba7e862b633a40281c24243c07421c671bf3e978",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b503684a355f-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "1 criminal defendant who unlike Petitioner was not\n2 indigent, I don't see any reason why the defendant\n3 wouldn't be able to file an amended notice of appeal,\n4 and many circuits don't require an additional fee.\n5 And if the fee issue is something that the\n6 Court is concerned about, that is the sort of thing that\n7 it would be appropriate for the rules committee to\n8 address."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice GINSBURG",
      "case_id": "2016-15-7250",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "b503684a355fd1e8fd71f3fbba7e862b633a40281c24243c07421c671bf3e978",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b503684a355f-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "9 JUSTICE GINSBURG: You're recognizing that\n10 he could file -- you could treat as an amended notice of\n11 appeal, so it's one notice of appeal but it's been\n12 amended."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. KEDEM",
      "case_id": "2016-15-7250",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "b503684a355fd1e8fd71f3fbba7e862b633a40281c24243c07421c671bf3e978",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b503684a355f-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "13 MR. KEDEM: I think you have to take a step\n14 to amend the notice of appeal, because, Justice\n15 Ginsburg, as you yourself pointed out, Appellate\n16 Rule 3(c)(1)(b) requires the notice of appeal to\n17 identify the judgment being appealed, and if you want to\n18 see the notice of appeal that Petitioner filed, I would\n19 direct you to page 42 of the joint appendix where he\n20 states very specifically that he is seeking appeal,\n21 quote, \"From the final judgment and sentence entered in\n22 this action on the 24th day of June, 2014.\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice KAGAN",
      "case_id": "2016-15-7250",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "b503684a355fd1e8fd71f3fbba7e862b633a40281c24243c07421c671bf3e978",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b503684a355f-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "2 JUSTICE KAGAN: Well, maybe this would be a\n3 good segue. This -- this -- you understand the\n4 requirement of a notice of appeal to be jurisdictional.\n5 Do you understand the requirement of timeliness to be\n6 jurisdictional or not?\n7 MR. KEDEM: Not in the criminal context."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. RASHKIND",
      "case_id": "2016-15-7250",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "b503684a355fd1e8fd71f3fbba7e862b633a40281c24243c07421c671bf3e978",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b503684a355f-0002-0009",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "1 has been clear in these rules from the beginning as to\n2 criminal cases, one notice of appeal is all that is\n3 required.\n4 Where Congress legislates in a way that\n5 creates this sort of anomaly where there might be two\n6 windows to appeal, it shouldn't change the underlying\n7 rules. They are that if you file a notice of appeal as\n8 to a type -- which is timely as to part of the sentence\n9 and judgment, that it remains timely as to all aspects\n10 of that as it is completed."
    },
